A||||8||Stoic||Can I avoid replacing motor?||||5||
Z||000000||Stoic||12-18-2000||04:59 PM||||I took my car to shop the other day after my exhaust manifold heated up to a bright red, and could not determine the sourse of the problem.  That, according to the mechanic, is the least of my problems.  I have been told my compression ratings are down to under 70psi (1st cyl) 90 (2nd) 105 (3rd) and 120 (4th).  Also, there is a very slight knock to it.  The car runs very rough, backfiring, stalling, jerking during acceleration.  I was told I have no choice but to replace the motor.  Is this probably the case or is it possibly the head gasket, or something else.  It's a 4cyl, auto.  Any opinions are would be appreciated.<BR>Thanks..<P>------------------<BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||12-18-2000||06:01 PM||||Check the usual suspects...timing, vac. leaks and EGR...all of which can cause the red-hot manifolds.<P>On occasion, the poor compression can be caused by excessive deposits on the valves and pistons (which can also cause the knock).<P>Try a decarbonizing kit (or use water...try searching the Internet for "decarbon procedure") and see if that improves things. <P>Your motor could be worn out...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sage||12-18-2000||06:45 PM||||How many miles?  Are you losing coolant? Are you getting coolant in the oil?(Will cause low coolant level and your oil will look foamy)  Look for a cracked head.  Kinda sounds like it may be time for a rebuild or an upgrade!<P>Have a good one!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||horseman||12-18-2000||07:36 PM||||Hey Stoic,<BR> <BR>For removing carbon try Sea Foam. Follow label directions. <BR>Double check timing for possible cause of red glowing manifolds. If this does not help than check for vacuum leaks as posted previously.<BR>Hope that this helps,<BR>Charlie||||reg||1
Z||000004||zeusrekning||12-18-2000||07:56 PM||||you can always give me your car? <BR>had to try<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-18-2000||08:07 PM||||check the head gasket. Adjacent low cylinders are a good sign the gasket fried. replace ALL the head bolts. <P>These 4 cyl have known issues with the factory head bolts on the exhaust side failing. Its usually the middle ones but any are candidates.<P>The fire recall in my cave lists some of the info about it. the rest is in a couple GM TSBs. GM upgraded the whole bolt set.<P>I believe these are "torque to yeild" bolts that have to be replaced every time the head is off. Even if they aren't the factory bolts must not be reused even on cars that have been thru the recall work.||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||12-18-2000||08:41 PM||||First you need to get a vacuum gauge and read the instructions on how to diagnose the motor that come with it. A vacuum gauge can tell you a lot about the engine’s problem.<P>You should do the compression test again but this time add a small teaspoon of motor oil to the low cylinders. If the compression goes up than your mechanic is probably right and the piston rings are shot. If the compression is not effected than you may have a bad valve, head gasket, or head. Usually when the head gasket is blown between cylinders the neighboring cylinders will both have low compression like your number one and two cylinders. <P>What you need to do is get a good book and do some thorough testing on your engine before you cough up the cash and time it takes to do an engine replacement or rebuild. Don't forget to check oil pressure too because that is a good indication of how the bottom end of your engine is.<P>Never just assume anything unless you like throwing money away.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Stoic||12-18-2000||09:34 PM||||Thanks alot for the help guys.  The engine has 260,000 kms on it.  I think it may be just tired, but I will try these suggestions first.  When it comes to the exhaust manifold, I discovered the vaccuum lines were hooked up wrong, and the oxygen sensor was bad.  No more red manifold, but as for the engine itself, I'm just hoping I don't have to replace it.<BR> Thanks again for the input guys.<P>------------------<BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FST2M6||12-18-2000||09:44 PM||||have you checked the easy stuff? the cat clogged? that will casue all the symptoms you just rattled off! lop it off and run a straight pipe out the side.. yes it'll be loud inspect the cat and if it has more than 50k miles on it replace it. with the cat off see if this aids in the engines ability to run at any rpm and with and without a load (eg. in gear or park) ||||reg||1
